/* NCERT Biology Class XII — Chapter 11: Organisms and Populations (book page 190–202) */
addChapter({
  id: 'bot-12-11',
  subj: 'bot',
  cls: 12,
  no: 11,
  title: 'Organisms and Populations',
  src: 'NCERT Biology XII · Chapter 11',
  points: [
    { id: 'in1', topic: 'Introduction', t: 'Levels of biological organisation: macromolecules, cells, tissues, organs, individual organisms, population, communities, ecosystems and biomes.' },
    { id: 'in2', topic: 'Introduction', t: 'At any level we can ask two types of questions: \'how-type\' questions seek the mechanism behind a process; \'why-type\' questions seek the significance (e.g. how/why does the bulbul sing).' },
    { id: 'in3', topic: 'Introduction', t: 'Ecology studies interactions among organisms and between the organism and its physical (abiotic) environment. Ecology is basically concerned with four levels — organisms, populations, communities and biomes.' },
    { id: 'pa1', topic: '11.1.1 Population Attributes', t: 'A population: individuals of a species living in a well-defined geographical area, sharing or competing for similar resources, potentially interbreeding. A group from asexual reproduction is also considered a population for ecological studies.' },
    { id: 'pa2', topic: '11.1.1 Population Attributes', t: 'Examples of populations: all cormorants in a wetland, rats in an abandoned dwelling, teakwood trees in a forest tract, bacteria in a culture plate, lotus plants in a pond.' },
    { id: 'pa3', topic: '11.1.1 Population Attributes', t: 'Natural selection operates at the population level to evolve desired traits. Population ecology is important because it links ecology to population genetics and evolution.' },
    { id: 'pa4', topic: '11.1.1 Population Attributes', t: 'A population has attributes an individual does not: birth rates and death rates, which are per capita births and deaths (change in numbers with respect to members of the population).' },
    { id: 'pa5', topic: '11.1.1 Population Attributes', t: 'Example: 20 lotus plants last year + 8 new by reproduction → birth rate 8/20 = 0.4 offspring per lotus per year. 4 of 40 fruitflies die in a week → death rate 4/40 = 0.1 individuals per fruitfly per week.' },
    { id: 'pa6', topic: '11.1.1 Population Attributes', t: 'Sex ratio is another population attribute — e.g. 60% females and 40% males.' },
    { id: 'pa7', topic: 'Age pyramids', t: 'Age distribution (per cent individuals of a given age group) plotted for a population gives an age pyramid (Fig 11.1); for humans it shows males and females. Shape shows growth status: expanding (growing), stable or declining. Age groups: pre-reproductive, reproductive, post-reproductive.' },
    { id: 'pd1', topic: 'Population size / density', t: 'Population size, technically called population density (N), tells its status in the habitat; ecological processes (competition, predation, pesticide effect) are evaluated as change in population size.' },
    { id: 'pd2', topic: 'Population size / density', t: 'Population size may be <10 (Siberian cranes at Bharatpur wetlands in any year) or go into millions (Chlamydomonas in a pond).' },
    { id: 'pd3', topic: 'Population size / density', t: 'Total number is generally the most appropriate measure, but sometimes meaningless — e.g. 200 Parthenium hysterophorus plants vs one huge banyan tree; here per cent cover or biomass is more meaningful.' },
    { id: 'pd4', topic: 'Population size / density', t: 'Sometimes relative densities serve the purpose — e.g. number of fish caught per trap. Population sizes are mostly estimated indirectly; tiger census in national parks is often based on pug marks and fecal pellets.' },
    { id: 'pg1', topic: '11.1.2 Population Growth', t: 'Population size is not static; it changes with food availability, predation pressure and adverse weather. Density fluctuates due to four basic processes: natality and immigration increase it; mortality and emigration decrease it.' },
    { id: 'pg2', topic: '11.1.2 Population Growth', t: 'Natality = number of births in a given period added to initial density; Mortality = number of deaths in a given period; Immigration = individuals of the same species that come into the habitat from elsewhere; Emigration = individuals who left the habitat.' },
    { id: 'pg3', topic: '11.1.2 Population Growth', t: 'Nt+1 = Nt + [(B + I) − (D + E)] (Fig 11.2). Population density increases if births + immigrants (B + I) are more than deaths + emigrants (D + E).' },
    { id: 'pg4', topic: '11.1.2 Population Growth', t: 'Under normal conditions births and deaths are the most important factors; immigration and emigration matter under special conditions — e.g. when a new habitat is being colonised, immigration may contribute more than birth rates.' },
    { id: 'ex1', topic: 'Growth models — Exponential', t: 'When resources (food, space) are unlimited, each species can realise its innate potential to grow (as Darwin observed); the population grows in an exponential or geometric fashion.' },
    { id: 'ex2', topic: 'Growth models — Exponential', t: 'With per capita birth rate b and death rate d: dN/dt = (b − d) × N; let (b − d) = r, then dN/dt = rN. r is the \'intrinsic rate of natural increase\', an important parameter for assessing impact of biotic or abiotic factors.' },
    { id: 'ex3', topic: 'Growth models — Exponential', t: 'r values: Norway rat 0.015; flour beetle 0.12; human population in India (1981) 0.0205.' },
    { id: 'ex4', topic: 'Growth models — Exponential', t: 'Exponential growth gives a J-shaped curve of N vs time (Fig 11.3 a). Integral form: Nt = N0 e^rt (Nt = density after time t, N0 = density at time zero, e = 2.71828).' },
    { id: 'ex5', topic: 'Growth models — Exponential', t: 'Any species growing exponentially under unlimited resources can reach enormous densities in a short time; Darwin showed even a slow-growing elephant could reach enormous numbers without checks (chess-board wheat grain story; Paramecium doubling daily for 64 days).' },
    { id: 'lg1', topic: 'Growth models — Logistic', t: 'No population in nature has unlimited resources; this leads to competition for limited resources and the \'fittest\' individuals survive and reproduce. Governments also restrain human population growth.' },
    { id: 'lg2', topic: 'Growth models — Logistic', t: 'A habitat has resources for a maximum possible number beyond which no further growth is possible — nature\'s carrying capacity (K) for that species in that habitat.' },
    { id: 'lg3', topic: 'Growth models — Logistic', t: 'Population in a habitat with limited resources shows a lag phase, then acceleration and deceleration, and finally an asymptote when density reaches carrying capacity — a sigmoid curve: Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth (Fig 11.3 b).' },
    { id: 'lg4', topic: 'Growth models — Logistic', t: 'Logistic growth equation: dN/dt = rN((K − N)/K), where N = population density at time t, r = intrinsic rate of natural increase, K = carrying capacity.' },
    { id: 'lg5', topic: 'Growth models — Logistic', t: 'Since resources for most animal populations are finite and become limiting sooner or later, the logistic growth model is considered more realistic.' },
    { id: 'lh1', topic: '11.1.3 Life History Variation', t: 'Populations evolve to maximise reproductive fitness, also called Darwinian fitness (high r value), in their habitat; under a set of selection pressures organisms evolve the most efficient reproductive strategy.' },
    { id: 'lh2', topic: '11.1.3 Life History Variation', t: 'Some organisms breed only once in their lifetime (Pacific salmon fish, bamboo); others breed many times (most birds and mammals).' },
    { id: 'lh3', topic: '11.1.3 Life History Variation', t: 'Some produce a large number of small-sized offspring (oysters, pelagic fishes); others a small number of large-sized offspring (birds, mammals).' },
    { id: 'lh4', topic: '11.1.3 Life History Variation', t: 'Life history traits have evolved in relation to constraints imposed by abiotic and biotic components of the habitat; evolution of life history traits is an important area of research.' },
    { id: 'pi1', topic: '11.1.4 Population Interactions', t: 'No habitat is inhabited by a single species. Even a plant needs soil microbes to break down organic matter and return inorganic nutrients, and animal agents for pollination; plants, animals and microbes interact to form a biological community.' },
    { id: 'pi2', topic: '11.1.4 Population Interactions', t: 'Interspecific interactions arise between populations of two different species; they may be beneficial (+), detrimental (−) or neutral (0) to one or both species (Table 11.1).' },
    { id: 'pi3', topic: 'Table 11.1', t: 'Table 11.1: Mutualism (+,+); Competition (−,−); Predation (+,−); Parasitism (+,−); Commensalism (+,0); Amensalism (−,0).' },
    { id: 'pi4', topic: 'Table 11.1', t: 'In parasitism and predation only one species benefits (parasite, predator) and the interaction is detrimental to the other (host, prey). Commensalism: one benefits, other neither benefited nor harmed. Amensalism: one harmed, other unaffected.' },
    { id: 'pi5', topic: 'Table 11.1', t: 'Predation, parasitism and commensalism share a common characteristic — the interacting species live closely together.' },
    { id: 'pr1', topic: '(i) Predation', t: 'Predation is nature\'s way of transferring to higher trophic levels the energy fixed by plants. A sparrow eating any seed is no less a predator; herbivores are, in a broad ecological context, not very different from predators.' },
    { id: 'pr2', topic: '(i) Predation', t: 'Predators act as \'conduits\' for energy transfer and keep prey populations under control; without them prey could reach very high densities causing ecosystem instability.' },
    { id: 'pr3', topic: '(i) Predation', t: 'Exotic species introduced into a new area become invasive because the invaded land lacks natural predators. Prickly pear cactus introduced into Australia in the early 1920s spread over millions of hectares; controlled only after a cactus-feeding predator (a moth) was introduced from its natural habitat.' },
    { id: 'pr4', topic: '(i) Predation', t: 'Biological control methods in agricultural pest control are based on the ability of the predator to regulate prey population.' },
    { id: 'pr5', topic: '(i) Predation', t: 'Predators maintain species diversity by reducing competition among competing prey species. When starfish Pisaster was removed from an intertidal area of the American Pacific Coast, more than 10 invertebrate species became extinct within a year due to inter-specific competition.' },
    { id: 'pr6', topic: '(i) Predation', t: 'If a predator overexploits its prey, the prey may become extinct and then the predator too — hence predators in nature are \'prudent\'.' },
    { id: 'pr7', topic: 'Prey defences', t: 'Prey defences: some insects and frogs are cryptically coloured (camouflaged); some are poisonous. The Monarch butterfly is highly distasteful to its predator (bird) due to a chemical it acquires during its caterpillar stage by feeding on a poisonous weed.' },
    { id: 'pr8', topic: 'Plant defences', t: 'For plants, herbivores are the predators. Nearly 25 per cent of all insects are phytophagous (feeding on plant sap and other parts). Plants cannot run away, so they evolved morphological and chemical defences.' },
    { id: 'pr9', topic: 'Plant defences', t: 'Thorns (Acacia, Cactus) are the most common morphological defence. The weed Calotropis produces highly poisonous cardiac glycosides, so cattle and goats do not browse on it.' },
    { id: 'pr10', topic: 'Plant defences', t: 'Chemicals we extract from plants commercially — nicotine, caffeine, quinine, strychnine, opium etc. — are produced by plants as defences against grazers and browsers.' },
    { id: 'cm1', topic: '(ii) Competition', t: 'Darwin was convinced that interspecific competition is a potent force in organic evolution. It is believed competition occurs when closely related species compete for limiting resources, but this is not entirely true.' },
    { id: 'cm2', topic: '(ii) Competition', t: 'Totally unrelated species can also compete — e.g. in some shallow South American lakes visiting flamingoes and resident fishes compete for zooplankton.' },
    { id: 'cm3', topic: '(ii) Competition', t: 'Resources need not be limiting: in interference competition the feeding efficiency of one species may be reduced by the interfering presence of another even when resources are abundant.' },
    { id: 'cm4', topic: '(ii) Competition', t: 'Competition is best defined as a process in which the fitness of one species (measured by its \'r\', intrinsic rate of increase) is significantly lower in the presence of another species.' },
    { id: 'cm5', topic: '(ii) Competition', t: 'Gause and others showed in labs that with limited resources the competitively superior species eliminates the other; evidence of such competitive exclusion in nature is not always conclusive.' },
    { id: 'cm6', topic: '(ii) Competition', t: 'Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos Islands became extinct within a decade after goats were introduced, apparently due to the goats\' greater browsing efficiency.' },
    { id: 'cm7', topic: '(ii) Competition', t: 'Competitive release: a species restricted to a small area due to a competitively superior species expands its range dramatically when the competitor is removed. Connell: on rocky sea coasts of Scotland, the larger barnacle Balanus dominates the intertidal area and excludes the smaller barnacle Chathamalus.' },
    { id: 'cm8', topic: '(ii) Competition', t: 'In general, herbivores and plants appear to be more adversely affected by competition than carnivores.' },
    { id: 'cm9', topic: 'Competitive Exclusion Principle', t: 'Gause\'s Competitive Exclusion Principle: two closely related species competing for the same resources cannot co-exist indefinitely and the competitively inferior one is eventually eliminated — may be true if resources are limiting, not otherwise.' },
    { id: 'cm10', topic: 'Resource partitioning', t: 'Species facing competition may evolve mechanisms promoting co-existence, e.g. resource partitioning — different feeding times or foraging patterns. MacArthur showed five closely related species of warblers on the same tree co-exist due to behavioural differences in foraging.' },
    { id: 'pt1', topic: '(iii) Parasitism', t: 'Parasitism ensures free lodging and meals, so it evolved in many groups from plants to higher vertebrates. Many parasites are host-specific (parasitise a single host species); host and parasite tend to co-evolve.' },
    { id: 'pt2', topic: '(iii) Parasitism', t: 'Parasite adaptations: loss of unnecessary sense organs, adhesive organs or suckers, loss of digestive system and high reproductive capacity.' },
    { id: 'pt3', topic: '(iii) Parasitism', t: 'Life cycles are often complex with intermediate hosts or vectors: human liver fluke (a trematode) depends on two intermediate hosts (a snail and a fish); the malarial parasite needs a vector (mosquito).' },
    { id: 'pt4', topic: '(iii) Parasitism', t: 'Majority of parasites harm the host — reduce survival, growth, reproduction and population density, and may make the host more vulnerable to predation by weakening it.' },
    { id: 'pt5', topic: 'Ectoparasites', t: 'Ectoparasites feed on the external surface of the host — lice on humans, ticks on dogs, ectoparasitic copepods on marine fish.' },
    { id: 'pt6', topic: 'Ectoparasites', t: 'Cuscuta, a parasitic plant on hedge plants, has lost its chlorophyll and leaves during evolution and derives nutrition from the host plant. The female mosquito is not considered a parasite although it needs our blood for reproduction.' },
    { id: 'pt7', topic: 'Endoparasites', t: 'Endoparasites live inside the host body (liver, kidney, lungs, RBCs etc.); their life cycles are more complex due to extreme specialisation; morphological and anatomical features greatly simplified while emphasising reproductive potential.' },
    { id: 'pt8', topic: 'Brood parasitism', t: 'Brood parasitism in birds: the parasitic bird lays eggs in the nest of its host and lets the host incubate them; its eggs have evolved to resemble the host\'s eggs in size and colour. Example: cuckoo (koel) and crow, breeding season spring to summer.' },
    { id: 'co1', topic: '(iv) Commensalism', t: 'Commensalism: one species benefits, the other is neither harmed nor benefited. Examples: orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch; barnacles growing on the back of a whale.' },
    { id: 'co2', topic: '(iv) Commensalism', t: 'Cattle egret and grazing cattle: egrets forage near grazing cattle because cattle stir up and flush out insects from vegetation.' },
    { id: 'co3', topic: '(iv) Commensalism', t: 'Sea anemone with stinging tentacles and clown fish living among them: the fish gets protection from predators; the anemone does not appear to derive any benefit.' },
    { id: 'mu1', topic: '(v) Mutualism', t: 'Mutualism confers benefits on both species. Lichens: intimate mutualism between a fungus and photosynthesising algae or cyanobacteria. Mycorrhizae: fungi help plant roots absorb nutrients; plant gives fungi energy-yielding carbohydrates.' },
    { id: 'mu2', topic: '(v) Mutualism', t: 'Most spectacular mutualism is in plant-animal relationships: plants offer pollen and nectar to pollinators and juicy fruits to seed dispersers as \'fees\'; the system must be safeguarded against \'cheaters\' (animals stealing nectar without pollinating).' },
    { id: 'mu3', topic: '(v) Mutualism', t: 'Plant-animal interactions often involve co-evolution — evolution of the flower and its pollinator are tightly linked.' },
    { id: 'mu4', topic: 'Fig and wasp', t: 'Many fig species have a tight one-to-one relationship with a pollinator wasp species (Fig 11.4). The female wasp uses the fruit as an oviposition site and uses developing seeds to nourish larvae; she pollinates the fig inflorescence while searching for egg-laying sites; the fig offers some seeds as food for larvae.' },
    { id: 'mu5', topic: 'Orchids', t: 'Orchids show a bewildering diversity of floral patterns to attract the right pollinator insect (bees, bumblebees) (Fig 11.5). Not all orchids offer rewards.' },
    { id: 'mu6', topic: 'Orchids — Ophrys', t: 'Mediterranean orchid Ophrys uses \'sexual deceit\': one petal resembles the female bee in size, colour and markings; the male bee \'pseudocopulates\' with the flower, gets dusted with pollen and transfers it to another flower.' },
    { id: 'mu7', topic: 'Orchids — Ophrys', t: 'If the female bee\'s colour patterns change even slightly during evolution, pollination success falls unless the orchid flower co-evolves to maintain the resemblance — an example of co-evolution.' }
  ],
  qs: [
    { id: 'q1', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which is the correct ascending order of levels of biological organisation?', o: ['Cells → tissues → organs → organism → population → community → ecosystem → biome', 'Population → organism → community → ecosystem', 'Biome → ecosystem → community → population', 'Organism → cell → tissue → organ'], a: 0, e: 'Macromolecules, cells, tissues, organs, organisms, population, communities, ecosystems and biomes.' },
    { id: 'q2', p: ['in1', 'in3'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Biome is a level of biological organisation.', 'Ecology is basically concerned with four levels — organisms, populations, communities and biomes.'], a: 0, e: 'Both correct.' },
    { id: 'q3', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: '\'Why does the bulbul sing?\' is a question that seeks the:', o: ['Mechanism', 'Significance', 'Structure', 'Evolution only'], a: 1, e: '\'Why-type\' questions seek the significance; \'how-type\' seek the mechanism.' },
    { id: 'q4', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The answer "operation of the voice box and vibrating bone in the bird" answers which type of question?', o: ['Why-type', 'How-type', 'Where-type', 'When-type'], a: 1, e: 'It explains the mechanism — how-type.' },
    { id: 'q5', p: ['in3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Ecology is a subject which studies:', o: ['Only plants', 'Interactions among organisms and between the organism and its physical environment', 'Only fossils', 'Only genetics'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT definition.' },
    { id: 'q6', p: ['pa1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'A group of individuals of a species in a well defined area, sharing resources and potentially interbreeding, is a:', o: ['Community', 'Population', 'Ecosystem', 'Biome'], a: 1, e: 'They constitute a population.' },
    { id: 'q7', p: ['pa1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Bacteria in a culture plate constitute a population.', R: 'A group of individuals resulting from asexual reproduction is also considered a population for ecological studies.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q8', p: ['pa2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which of the following is NOT an example of a population?', o: ['All cormorants in a wetland', 'Lotus plants in a pond', 'Teakwood trees in a forest tract', 'All animals and plants in a forest'], a: 3, e: 'All animals and plants of different species form a community, not a population.' },
    { id: 'q9', p: ['pa3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Natural selection operates to evolve desired traits at the level of:', o: ['Individual', 'Population', 'Cell', 'Biome'], a: 1, e: 'It is at the population level that natural selection operates.' },
    { id: 'q10', p: ['pa3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Population ecology is an important area of study.', R: 'It links ecology to population genetics and evolution.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q11', p: ['pa4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Birth rates and death rates in a population refer to:', o: ['Total births and deaths', 'Per capita births and deaths', 'Births in a year only', 'Deaths of old individuals'], a: 1, e: 'These rates refer to per capita births and deaths.' },
    { id: 'q12', p: ['pa5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'A pond had 20 lotus plants last year; 8 new plants were added by reproduction. The birth rate is:', o: ['0.2', '0.4', '0.8', '2.5'], a: 1, e: '8/20 = 0.4 offspring per lotus per year.' },
    { id: 'q13', p: ['pa5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'If 4 individuals in a laboratory population of 40 fruitflies die in a week, the death rate is:', o: ['0.1 per fruitfly per week', '0.4 per week', '10 per week', '4 per week'], a: 0, e: '4/40 = 0.1 individuals per fruitfly per week.' },
    { id: 'q14', p: ['pa5', 'pa4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'A population of 50 frogs gives 15 new frogs in a year. The birth rate is:', o: ['0.15', '0.3', '3.3', '15'], a: 1, e: '15/50 = 0.3 per frog per year.' },
    { id: 'q15', p: ['pa6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which of these is an attribute of a population and NOT of an individual?', o: ['Being male', 'Sex ratio', 'Age', 'Body size'], a: 1, e: 'An individual is male or female, but a population has a sex ratio.' },
    { id: 'q16', p: ['pa6', 'pa4'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which of the following are population attributes? A. Birth rate B. Death rate C. Sex ratio D. Age distribution', o: ['A and B', 'A, B and C', 'C and D', 'A, B, C and D'], a: 3, e: 'All are population attributes.' },
    { id: 'q17', p: ['pa7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'When the age distribution of a population is plotted, the resulting structure is called:', o: ['Ecological pyramid', 'Age pyramid', 'Food web', 'Growth curve'], a: 1, e: 'It is called an age pyramid (Fig 11.1).' },
    { id: 'q18', p: ['pa7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'An age pyramid with a broad base of pre-reproductive individuals indicates a population that is:', o: ['Declining', 'Stable', 'Expanding (growing)', 'Extinct'], a: 2, e: 'Fig 11.1: expanding pyramid has the broadest pre-reproductive base.' },
    { id: 'q19', p: ['pa7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In a declining population\'s age pyramid, the pre-reproductive group is:', o: ['Largest', 'Smaller than the reproductive group', 'Equal to post-reproductive group always', 'Absent'], a: 1, e: 'Fig 11.1: declining pyramid has a narrow pre-reproductive base.' },
    { id: 'q20', p: ['pd1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Population size is technically called:', o: ['Population density (N)', 'Carrying capacity (K)', 'Intrinsic rate (r)', 'Biomass'], a: 0, e: 'Population size, technically called population density (designated as N).' },
    { id: 'q21', p: ['pd1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Effects of competition, predation or pesticides on a population are evaluated in terms of change in population size.', 'Population density must always be measured in numbers only.'], a: 2, e: 'Statement II false — need not be measured in numbers only.' },
    { id: 'q22', p: ['pd2'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the population with its typical size.', match: { h: ['List I', 'List II'], l: ['Siberian cranes at Bharatpur', 'Chlamydomonas in a pond'], r: ['Millions', 'Less than 10'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I', 'A-I, B-II', 'A-I, B-I', 'A-II, B-II'], a: 0, e: 'Siberian cranes <10; Chlamydomonas in millions.' },
    { id: 'q23', p: ['pd2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Siberian cranes visit which wetland in India in numbers less than 10?', o: ['Chilika', 'Bharatpur', 'Sundarbans', 'Loktak'], a: 1, e: 'Siberian cranes at Bharatpur wetlands.' },
    { id: 'q24', p: ['pd3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In an area with 200 Parthenium plants and one huge banyan tree, the more meaningful measure of population size of banyan is:', o: ['Total number', 'Per cent cover or biomass', 'Birth rate', 'Sex ratio'], a: 1, e: 'Per cent cover or biomass is a more meaningful measure.' },
    { id: 'q25', p: ['pd3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Total number is not always an appropriate measure of population density.', R: 'Counting a huge banyan as one plant underestimates its enormous role in the community.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT example.' },
    { id: 'q26', p: ['pd4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Tiger census in our national parks is often based on:', o: ['Direct counting', 'Pug marks and fecal pellets', 'Satellite images only', 'Blood samples'], a: 1, e: 'Based on pug marks and fecal pellets.' },
    { id: 'q27', p: ['pd4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Number of fish caught per trap is an example of:', o: ['Absolute density', 'Relative density', 'Natality', 'Carrying capacity'], a: 1, e: 'Relative densities serve the purpose equally well.' },
    { id: 'q28', p: ['pg1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which processes contribute to an increase in population density?', o: ['Natality and immigration', 'Mortality and emigration', 'Natality and emigration', 'Mortality and immigration'], a: 0, e: 'Natality and immigration increase; mortality and emigration decrease.' },
    { id: 'q29', p: ['pg1'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 1, q: 'Population size changes depending on: A. Food availability B. Predation pressure C. Adverse weather', o: ['A only', 'A and B', 'B and C', 'A, B and C'], a: 3, e: 'All three are mentioned.' },
    { id: 'q30', p: ['pg2'], type: 'match', lvl: 1, q: 'Match the term with its meaning.', match: { h: ['List I', 'List II'], l: ['Natality', 'Mortality', 'Immigration', 'Emigration'], r: ['Individuals leaving the habitat', 'Deaths in a period', 'Births in a period', 'Individuals coming into the habitat'] }, o: ['A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I', 'A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I', 'A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 0, e: 'Natality births; mortality deaths; immigration in; emigration out.' },
    { id: 'q31', p: ['pg2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Number of individuals of the same species that have come into the habitat from elsewhere is:', o: ['Natality', 'Immigration', 'Emigration', 'Mortality'], a: 1, e: 'Immigration.' },
    { id: 'q32', p: ['pg3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'If N is population density at time t, the density at time t+1 is:', o: ['Nt+1 = Nt + [(B + I) − (D + E)]', 'Nt+1 = Nt + [(B + E) − (D + I)]', 'Nt+1 = Nt − (B + D)', 'Nt+1 = Nt × r'], a: 0, e: 'Fig 11.2 equation.' },
    { id: 'q33', p: ['pg3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'Nt = 100, births = 20, immigrants = 5, deaths = 10, emigrants = 15. Nt+1 is:', o: ['100', '110', '90', '150'], a: 0, e: '100 + (20 + 5) − (10 + 15) = 100.' },
    { id: 'q34', p: ['pg4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'When a new habitat is just being colonised, the factor that may contribute more significantly to population growth is:', o: ['Birth rate', 'Immigration', 'Mortality', 'Emigration'], a: 1, e: 'Immigration may contribute more significantly than birth rates.' },
    { id: 'q35', p: ['pg4'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Under normal conditions, births and deaths are the most important factors influencing population density.', 'Immigration and emigration assume importance only under special conditions.'], a: 0, e: 'Both correct.' },
    { id: 'q36', p: ['ex1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'When resources in the habitat are unlimited, a population grows in:', o: ['Logistic fashion', 'Exponential or geometric fashion', 'Sigmoid fashion', 'Linear fashion'], a: 1, e: 'Exponential or geometric fashion.' },
    { id: 'q37', p: ['ex1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A species can realise fully its innate potential to grow in number only when resources are unlimited.', R: 'Resource (food and space) availability is essential for unimpeded growth of a population.' }, a: 0, e: 'As Darwin observed.' },
    { id: 'q38', p: ['ex2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In the exponential growth equation dN/dt = rN, r is called:', o: ['Carrying capacity', 'Intrinsic rate of natural increase', 'Mortality rate', 'Biotic potential constant e'], a: 1, e: 'r is the intrinsic rate of natural increase.' },
    { id: 'q39', p: ['ex2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'If b is per capita birth rate and d is per capita death rate, then r equals:', o: ['b + d', 'b − d', 'b × d', 'b / d'], a: 1, e: 'Let (b − d) = r.' },
    { id: 'q40', p: ['ex3'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the organism with its r value.', match: { h: ['List I', 'List II'], l: ['Norway rat', 'Flour beetle', 'Human population in India (1981)'], r: ['0.0205', '0.015', '0.12'] }, o: ['A-II, B-III, C-I', 'A-III, B-II, C-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II', 'A-II, B-I, C-III'], a: 0, e: 'Norway rat 0.015; flour beetle 0.12; India 1981 0.0205.' },
    { id: 'q41', p: ['ex3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In 1981, the r value for human population in India was:', o: ['0.015', '0.12', '0.0205', '1.15'], a: 2, e: 'In 1981, the r value for human population in India was 0.0205.' },
    { id: 'q42', p: ['ex4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Exponential growth results in a ____ curve when N is plotted against time.', o: ['S-shaped', 'J-shaped', 'Bell-shaped', 'Straight line'], a: 1, e: 'It results in a J-shaped curve.' },
    { id: 'q43', p: ['ex4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The integral form of the exponential growth equation is:', o: ['Nt = N0 e^rt', 'Nt = N0 + rt', 'Nt = K/(1 + e^rt)', 'Nt = N0 − e^rt'], a: 0, e: 'Nt = N0 e^rt.' },
    { id: 'q44', p: ['ex4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In Nt = N0 e^rt, e is the base of natural logarithms equal to:', o: ['2.71828', '3.14159', '1.414', '10'], a: 0, e: 'e = the base of natural logarithms (2.71828).' },
    { id: 'q45', p: ['ex5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Who showed that even a slow growing animal like the elephant could reach enormous numbers in the absence of checks?', o: ['Malthus', 'Darwin', 'Gause', 'MacArthur'], a: 1, e: 'Darwin showed this.' },
    { id: 'q46', p: ['ex5'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A Paramecium doubling every day would reach a mind-boggling size in 64 days with unlimited resources.', R: 'Populations growing exponentially can reach enormous densities in a short time.' }, a: 0, e: 'Illustrated by the chess-board wheat story.' },
    { id: 'q47', p: ['lg1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Competition between individuals for limited resources leads to:', o: ['Exponential growth', 'Survival and reproduction of the \'fittest\'', 'Unlimited growth', 'No change'], a: 1, e: 'Eventually the \'fittest\' individual will survive and reproduce.' },
    { id: 'q48', p: ['lg1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['No population in nature has unlimited resources for exponential growth.', 'Governments of many countries have introduced restraints to limit human population growth.'], a: 0, e: 'Both correct.' },
    { id: 'q49', p: ['lg2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The maximum population size that a habitat can support is called:', o: ['Biotic potential', 'Carrying capacity (K)', 'Intrinsic rate', 'Natality'], a: 1, e: 'Nature\'s carrying capacity (K).' },
    { id: 'q50', p: ['lg3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Logistic growth of a population results in a:', o: ['J-shaped curve', 'Sigmoid curve', 'Straight line', 'Inverted curve'], a: 1, e: 'A plot of N in relation to time results in a sigmoid curve.' },
    { id: 'q51', p: ['lg3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Correct sequence of phases in logistic growth:', o: ['Acceleration → lag → asymptote → deceleration', 'Lag → acceleration → deceleration → asymptote', 'Asymptote → lag → acceleration', 'Deceleration → acceleration → lag'], a: 1, e: 'Lag phase, then acceleration and deceleration, and finally an asymptote at carrying capacity.' },
    { id: 'q52', p: ['lg3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Logistic growth is also called:', o: ['Darwinian growth', 'Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth', 'Malthusian growth', 'Geometric growth'], a: 1, e: 'Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth.' },
    { id: 'q53', p: ['lg4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The logistic growth equation is:', o: ['dN/dt = rN', 'dN/dt = rN((K − N)/K)', 'dN/dt = rK', 'dN/dt = N(K − r)'], a: 1, e: 'dN/dt = rN((K − N)/K).' },
    { id: 'q54', p: ['lg4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'In logistic growth, when N = K, the growth rate dN/dt becomes:', o: ['Maximum', 'rN', 'Zero', 'Negative infinity'], a: 2, e: '(K − N)/K = 0, so dN/dt = 0 — population at asymptote.' },
    { id: 'q55', p: ['lg4', 'lg2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'For a population with r = 0.1, K = 1000 and N = 500, dN/dt is:', o: ['50', '25', '100', '0'], a: 1, e: '0.1 × 500 × (500/1000) = 25.' },
    { id: 'q56', p: ['lg5'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The logistic growth model is considered more realistic.', R: 'Resources for growth of most animal populations are finite and become limiting sooner or later.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q57', p: ['lg5', 'ex4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Exponential growth is considered more realistic for most animal populations.', 'Logistic growth curve has an asymptote at carrying capacity.'], a: 3, e: 'Statement I false — logistic is more realistic. Statement II true.' },
    { id: 'q58', p: ['lh1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Reproductive fitness (high r value) is also called:', o: ['Mendelian fitness', 'Darwinian fitness', 'Physical fitness', 'Carrying capacity'], a: 1, e: 'Reproductive fitness, also called Darwinian fitness (high r value).' },
    { id: 'q59', p: ['lh1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Organisms evolve towards the most efficient reproductive strategy.', R: 'Populations evolve to maximise their reproductive fitness in their habitat.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q60', p: ['lh2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which organisms breed only once in their lifetime?', o: ['Most birds and mammals', 'Pacific salmon fish and bamboo', 'Oysters', 'Humans'], a: 1, e: 'Pacific salmon fish, bamboo.' },
    { id: 'q61', p: ['lh2', 'lh3'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the life history pattern with examples.', match: { h: ['List I', 'List II'], l: ['Breed only once', 'Breed many times', 'Many small offspring', 'Few large offspring'], r: ['Birds, mammals (offspring size)', 'Oysters, pelagic fishes', 'Most birds and mammals (breeding)', 'Pacific salmon, bamboo'] }, o: ['A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II', 'A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I'], a: 0, e: 'Once — salmon, bamboo; many times — birds/mammals; many small — oysters/pelagic fish; few large — birds/mammals.' },
    { id: 'q62', p: ['lh3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which organisms produce a large number of small-sized offspring?', o: ['Birds', 'Mammals', 'Oysters and pelagic fishes', 'Elephants'], a: 2, e: 'Oysters, pelagic fishes.' },
    { id: 'q63', p: ['lh4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Life history traits of organisms have evolved in relation to:', o: ['Only temperature', 'Constraints imposed by abiotic and biotic components of the habitat', 'Human choice', 'Random chance only'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q64', p: ['lh4'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Evolution of life history traits is an important area of research by ecologists.', 'Life history traits are independent of the habitat.'], a: 2, e: 'Statement II false.' },
    { id: 'q65', p: ['pi1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For any species, the minimal requirement is:', o: ['Absence of all other species', 'One more species on which it can feed', 'Only abiotic factors', 'A predator'], a: 1, e: 'For any species, the minimal requirement is one more species on which it can feed.' },
    { id: 'q66', p: ['pi1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Even a plant species that makes its own food cannot survive alone.', R: 'It needs soil microbes to return inorganic nutrients and animal agents for pollination.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q67', p: ['pi2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In Table 11.1, the sign \'0\' represents an interaction that is:', o: ['Beneficial', 'Detrimental', 'Neutral', 'Lethal'], a: 2, e: '\'+\' beneficial, \'−\' detrimental, \'0\' neutral.' },
    { id: 'q68', p: ['pi3'], type: 'match', lvl: 1, q: 'Match the interaction with its sign (Species A, Species B).', match: { h: ['List I', 'List II'], l: ['Mutualism', 'Competition', 'Commensalism', 'Amensalism'], r: ['−, 0', '+, 0', '+, +', '−, −'] }, o: ['A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I'], a: 0, e: 'Mutualism ++, competition −−, commensalism +0, amensalism −0.' },
    { id: 'q69', p: ['pi3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which interactions are represented by (+, −)?', o: ['Mutualism and commensalism', 'Predation and parasitism', 'Competition and amensalism', 'Only predation'], a: 1, e: 'Predation and parasitism are (+, −).' },
    { id: 'q70', p: ['pi4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In amensalism:', o: ['Both benefit', 'One species is harmed whereas the other is unaffected', 'Both are harmed', 'One benefits, other unaffected'], a: 1, e: 'In amensalism one species is harmed whereas the other is unaffected.' },
    { id: 'q71', p: ['pi4'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Both species benefit in mutualism.', 'Both species lose in competition.'], a: 0, e: 'Both correct.' },
    { id: 'q72', p: ['pi5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Predation, parasitism and commensalism share the common characteristic that:', o: ['Both species benefit', 'The interacting species live closely together', 'Both species are harmed', 'They occur only in plants'], a: 1, e: 'The interacting species live closely together.' },
    { id: 'q73', p: ['pr1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Predation is nature\'s way of:', o: ['Producing energy', 'Transferring energy fixed by plants to higher trophic levels', 'Increasing prey numbers', 'Decomposing matter'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q74', p: ['pr1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A sparrow eating seeds is a predator.', R: 'In a broad ecological context, animals eating plants are not very different from predators.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q75', p: ['pr2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In the absence of predators, prey species could:', o: ['Become extinct immediately', 'Achieve very high population densities and cause ecosystem instability', 'Stop reproducing', 'Become predators'], a: 1, e: 'Prey could achieve very high densities causing instability.' },
    { id: 'q76', p: ['pr2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Predators act as \'conduits\' for:', o: ['Water transfer', 'Energy transfer across trophic levels', 'Gene transfer', 'Pollination'], a: 1, e: 'They act as conduits for energy transfer across trophic levels.' },
    { id: 'q77', p: ['pr3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Prickly pear cactus introduced into Australia was brought under control by introducing:', o: ['A goat', 'A cactus-feeding moth', 'A fungus', 'A virus'], a: 1, e: 'A cactus-feeding predator (a moth) from its natural habitat.' },
    { id: 'q78', p: ['pr3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Exotic species introduced into a new area often become invasive.', R: 'The invaded land does not have their natural predators.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q79', p: ['pr3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Prickly pear cactus was introduced into Australia in:', o: ['Early 1900s', 'Early 1920s', '1950s', '1990s'], a: 1, e: 'In the early 1920\'s.' },
    { id: 'q80', p: ['pr4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Biological control methods in agricultural pest control are based on:', o: ['Use of pesticides', 'The ability of the predator to regulate prey population', 'Crop rotation only', 'Genetic engineering only'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q81', p: ['pr5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In the American Pacific Coast intertidal communities, the important predator starfish is:', o: ['Balanus', 'Pisaster', 'Chathamalus', 'Ophrys'], a: 1, e: 'The starfish Pisaster.' },
    { id: 'q82', p: ['pr5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'When starfish were removed from an enclosed intertidal area:', o: ['Species diversity increased', 'More than 10 species of invertebrates became extinct within a year due to inter-specific competition', 'Nothing changed', 'Barnacles disappeared first'], a: 1, e: 'More than 10 species of invertebrates became extinct within a year.' },
    { id: 'q83', p: ['pr5'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Predators help maintain species diversity in a community.', R: 'Predators reduce the intensity of competition among competing prey species.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q84', p: ['pr6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Predators in nature are described as \'prudent\' because:', o: ['They hunt only at night', 'Overexploiting prey would make prey and then predator extinct', 'They eat plants too', 'They migrate'], a: 1, e: 'If a predator overexploits its prey, both become extinct.' },
    { id: 'q85', p: ['pr6'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['An overly efficient predator may drive its prey to extinction.', 'Extinction of prey has no effect on the predator.'], a: 2, e: 'Statement II false — predator also becomes extinct for lack of food.' },
    { id: 'q86', p: ['pr7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Some insects and frogs avoid detection by predators by being:', o: ['Brightly coloured', 'Cryptically coloured (camouflaged)', 'Larger', 'Nocturnal only'], a: 1, e: 'Cryptically-coloured (camouflaged).' },
    { id: 'q87', p: ['pr7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Monarch butterfly is distasteful to birds because of a chemical acquired:', o: ['From nectar as an adult', 'During its caterpillar stage by feeding on a poisonous weed', 'From soil', 'Synthesised in its wings'], a: 1, e: 'It acquires this chemical during its caterpillar stage by feeding on a poisonous weed.' },
    { id: 'q88', p: ['pr8'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Nearly what percentage of all insects are phytophagous?', o: ['10%', '25%', '50%', '75%'], a: 1, e: 'Nearly 25 per cent.' },
    { id: 'q89', p: ['pr8'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Plants have evolved a variety of morphological and chemical defences against herbivores.', R: 'Unlike animals, plants cannot run away from their predators.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q90', p: ['pr9'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The most common morphological means of defence in plants is:', o: ['Thorns', 'Cardiac glycosides', 'Nicotine', 'Bright flowers'], a: 0, e: 'Thorns (Acacia, Cactus).' },
    { id: 'q91', p: ['pr9'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Cattle and goats do not browse on Calotropis because it produces:', o: ['Thorns', 'Highly poisonous cardiac glycosides', 'Caffeine', 'Sticky latex only'], a: 1, e: 'The plant produces highly poisonous cardiac glycosides.' },
    { id: 'q92', p: ['pr10'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which chemicals are produced by plants as defences against grazers and browsers? A. Nicotine B. Caffeine C. Quinine D. Strychnine E. Opium', o: ['A and B', 'A, B and C', 'C, D and E', 'A, B, C, D and E'], a: 3, e: 'All are listed in NCERT.' },
    { id: 'q93', p: ['pr10'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Many plant chemicals we extract commercially are actually produced by plants as defences.', 'Plant chemicals never affect herbivores.'], a: 2, e: 'Statement II false — they can make herbivores sick, inhibit feeding or even kill them.' },
    { id: 'q94', p: ['cm1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Who was convinced that interspecific competition is a potent force in organic evolution?', o: ['Gause', 'Darwin', 'Connell', 'MacArthur'], a: 1, e: 'Darwin.' },
    { id: 'q95', p: ['cm1', 'cm2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Competition occurs only between closely related species.', 'Totally unrelated species could also compete for the same resource.'], a: 3, e: 'Statement I false; statement II true.' },
    { id: 'q96', p: ['cm2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In some shallow South American lakes, visiting flamingoes and resident fishes compete for:', o: ['Space', 'Zooplankton', 'Phytoplankton', 'Oxygen'], a: 1, e: 'Their common food, the zooplankton.' },
    { id: 'q97', p: ['cm3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In interference competition:', o: ['Resources must be limiting', 'Feeding efficiency of one species is reduced by the presence of another, even if resources are abundant', 'Both species benefit', 'Only plants compete'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT definition.' },
    { id: 'q98', p: ['cm3', 'cm4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Resources need not be limiting for competition to occur.', R: 'In interference competition, feeding efficiency of one species may be reduced by the inhibitory presence of another species.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q99', p: ['cm4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Competition is best defined as a process in which the fitness of one species, measured as its ____, is significantly lower in the presence of another species.', o: ['K', 'r (intrinsic rate of increase)', 'Biomass', 'Natality'], a: 1, e: 'Fitness measured in terms of its \'r\'.' },
    { id: 'q100', p: ['cm5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In laboratory experiments with limited resources, Gause showed that:', o: ['Both species coexist', 'The competitively superior species eventually eliminates the other', 'Both go extinct', 'Resources increase'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q101', p: ['cm5'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Competitive exclusion is easy to demonstrate in laboratory experiments.', 'Evidence for competitive exclusion in nature is always conclusive.'], a: 2, e: 'Statement II false — not always conclusive.' },
    { id: 'q102', p: ['cm6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos Islands became extinct after the introduction of:', o: ['Rats', 'Goats', 'Cats', 'Pigs'], a: 1, e: 'Within a decade after goats were introduced.' },
    { id: 'q103', p: ['cm6'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Abingdon tortoise became extinct within a decade of introduction of goats.', R: 'Goats had greater browsing efficiency than the tortoise.' }, a: 0, e: 'Circumstantial evidence of competition.' },
    { id: 'q104', p: ['cm7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Expansion of a species\' range when its competitively superior competitor is removed is called:', o: ['Resource partitioning', 'Competitive release', 'Competitive exclusion', 'Co-evolution'], a: 1, e: 'Competitive release.' },
    { id: 'q105', p: ['cm7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Connell\'s field experiments on rocky sea coasts of Scotland showed that:', o: ['Chathamalus dominates and excludes Balanus', 'The larger barnacle Balanus dominates the intertidal area and excludes the smaller Chathamalus', 'Both barnacles coexist equally', 'Starfish excludes barnacles'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q106', p: ['cm8'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In general, which are more adversely affected by competition?', o: ['Carnivores', 'Herbivores and plants', 'Decomposers', 'Parasites'], a: 1, e: 'Herbivores and plants appear to be more adversely affected than carnivores.' },
    { id: 'q107', p: ['cm8', 'cm7'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Carnivores are more adversely affected by competition than herbivores.', 'Balanus is larger than Chathamalus.'], a: 3, e: 'Statement I false; II true.' },
    { id: 'q108', p: ['cm9'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The Competitive Exclusion Principle was given by:', o: ['Darwin', 'Gause', 'MacArthur', 'Connell'], a: 1, e: 'Gause\'s Competitive Exclusion Principle.' },
    { id: 'q109', p: ['cm9'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Gause\'s principle states that two closely related species competing for the same resources:', o: ['Always coexist', 'Cannot co-exist indefinitely; the competitively inferior one is eliminated', 'Become mutualists', 'Both become extinct'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT statement — true if resources are limiting.' },
    { id: 'q110', p: ['cm10'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'MacArthur showed that five closely related species of ____ living on the same tree could coexist.', o: ['Barnacles', 'Warblers', 'Finches', 'Wasps'], a: 1, e: 'Five closely related species of warblers.' },
    { id: 'q111', p: ['cm10'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Coexistence of competing species by choosing different times for feeding or different foraging patterns is called:', o: ['Competitive exclusion', 'Resource partitioning', 'Brood parasitism', 'Amensalism'], a: 1, e: 'Resource partitioning.' },
    { id: 'q112', p: ['cm10', 'cm9'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'Five closely related warbler species coexist on the same tree.', R: 'Gause\'s competitive exclusion principle says closely related competing species can never coexist.' }, a: 2, e: 'A is true. R is false — the principle applies when resources are limiting; species may evolve resource partitioning (warblers differ in foraging behaviour).' },
    { id: 'q113', p: ['pt1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Many parasites are host-specific. This means they:', o: ['Parasitise many species', 'Can parasitise only a single species of host', 'Do not harm the host', 'Live freely'], a: 1, e: 'They can parasitise only a single species of host.' },
    { id: 'q114', p: ['pt1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Host and parasite tend to co-evolve.', R: 'If the host evolves mechanisms to resist the parasite, the parasite must evolve mechanisms to counteract them.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q115', p: ['pt2'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Special adaptations of parasites include: A. Loss of unnecessary sense organs B. Adhesive organs or suckers C. Loss of digestive system D. High reproductive capacity', o: ['A only', 'A and B', 'A, B and C', 'A, B, C and D'], a: 3, e: 'All four are given.' },
    { id: 'q116', p: ['pt3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The human liver fluke depends on which two intermediate hosts?', o: ['Mosquito and pig', 'A snail and a fish', 'Dog and cat', 'Cow and snail'], a: 1, e: 'Two intermediate hosts (a snail and a fish).' },
    { id: 'q117', p: ['pt3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The malarial parasite needs a vector to spread. The vector is:', o: ['Housefly', 'Mosquito', 'Snail', 'Tick'], a: 1, e: 'The malarial parasite needs a vector (mosquito).' },
    { id: 'q118', p: ['pt3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The human liver fluke is a:', o: ['Nematode', 'Trematode', 'Cestode', 'Protozoan'], a: 1, e: 'Human liver fluke (a trematode parasite).' },
    { id: 'q119', p: ['pt4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Majority of parasites:', o: ['Benefit the host', 'Harm the host — reduce its survival, growth, reproduction and population density', 'Are neutral', 'Increase host growth'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q120', p: ['pt4'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Parasites may render the host more vulnerable to predation by making it physically weak.', 'Parasites increase the population density of the host.'], a: 2, e: 'Statement II false.' },
    { id: 'q121', p: ['pt5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Parasites that feed on the external surface of the host are called:', o: ['Endoparasites', 'Ectoparasites', 'Brood parasites', 'Commensals'], a: 1, e: 'Ectoparasites.' },
    { id: 'q122', p: ['pt5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which of the following is an ectoparasite?', o: ['Liver fluke', 'Lice on humans', 'Malarial parasite', 'Tapeworm'], a: 1, e: 'Lice on humans, ticks on dogs, copepods on fish.' },
    { id: 'q123', p: ['pt6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Cuscuta, a parasitic plant, has lost its:', o: ['Roots only', 'Chlorophyll and leaves', 'Flowers', 'Stem'], a: 1, e: 'It has lost its chlorophyll and leaves in the course of evolution.' },
    { id: 'q124', p: ['pt6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Cuscuta is commonly found growing on:', o: ['Hedge plants', 'Cactus', 'Water hyacinth', 'Rice'], a: 0, e: 'Commonly found growing on hedge plants.' },
    { id: 'q125', p: ['pt6'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The female mosquito needs our blood for reproduction.', 'The female mosquito is considered a parasite.'], a: 2, e: 'Statement II false — it is not considered a parasite.' },
    { id: 'q126', p: ['pt7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Parasites living inside the host body (liver, kidney, lungs, RBCs) are:', o: ['Ectoparasites', 'Endoparasites', 'Epiphytes', 'Commensals'], a: 1, e: 'Endoparasites.' },
    { id: 'q127', p: ['pt7'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Life cycles of endoparasites are more complex.', R: 'Endoparasites show extreme specialisation.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q128', p: ['pt8'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Cuckoo laying eggs in the nest of a crow is an example of:', o: ['Commensalism', 'Brood parasitism', 'Mutualism', 'Predation'], a: 1, e: 'Brood parasitism in birds.' },
    { id: 'q129', p: ['pt8'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Eggs of the parasitic bird resemble the host\'s eggs in size and colour.', R: 'This reduces the chances of the host detecting the foreign eggs and ejecting them.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q130', p: ['co1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'An orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch is an example of:', o: ['Parasitism', 'Commensalism', 'Mutualism', 'Amensalism'], a: 1, e: 'Commensalism.' },
    { id: 'q131', p: ['co1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Barnacles growing on the back of a whale is an example of:', o: ['Commensalism', 'Parasitism', 'Predation', 'Competition'], a: 0, e: 'Barnacles benefit; whale derives no apparent benefit.' },
    { id: 'q132', p: ['co2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Cattle egret and grazing cattle is a classic example of:', o: ['Mutualism', 'Commensalism', 'Predation', 'Parasitism'], a: 1, e: 'Classic example of commensalism.' },
    { id: 'q133', p: ['co2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Egrets forage close to where cattle are grazing.', R: 'Cattle, as they move, flush out insects from vegetation.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q134', p: ['co3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In the interaction between sea anemone and clown fish:', o: ['Both benefit', 'Clown fish gets protection; anemone derives no benefit', 'Anemone eats the clown fish', 'Both are harmed'], a: 1, e: 'Commensalism — fish gets protection from predators.' },
    { id: 'q135', p: ['co3', 'co1', 'mu1'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the example with the interaction.', match: { h: ['List I', 'List II'], l: ['Clown fish and sea anemone', 'Lichen', 'Cuckoo and crow', 'Balanus and Chathamalus'], r: ['Competition', 'Brood parasitism', 'Mutualism', 'Commensalism'] }, o: ['A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'], a: 0, e: 'Clown fish — commensalism; lichen — mutualism; cuckoo — brood parasitism; barnacles — competition.' },
    { id: 'q136', p: ['mu1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Lichens represent an intimate mutualistic relationship between:', o: ['Fungus and roots', 'Fungus and photosynthesising algae or cyanobacteria', 'Bacteria and roots', 'Two fungi'], a: 1, e: 'Between a fungus and photosynthesising algae or cyanobacteria.' },
    { id: 'q137', p: ['mu1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In mycorrhizae, the plant provides the fungi with:', o: ['Phosphorus', 'Energy-yielding carbohydrates', 'Water only', 'Protection'], a: 1, e: 'The plant in turn provides the fungi with energy-yielding carbohydrates.' },
    { id: 'q138', p: ['mu2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Plants offer which \'fees\' to seed dispersers?', o: ['Pollen', 'Nectar', 'Juicy and nutritious fruits', 'Shelter'], a: 2, e: 'Pollen and nectar for pollinators; juicy and nutritious fruits for seed dispersers.' },
    { id: 'q139', p: ['mu2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In plant-animal mutualism, \'cheaters\' are:', o: ['Plants without nectar', 'Animals that steal nectar without aiding in pollination', 'Predators of pollinators', 'Seed dispersers'], a: 1, e: 'Animals that try to steal nectar without aiding in pollination.' },
    { id: 'q140', p: ['mu3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Plant-animal interactions often involve:', o: ['Competitive exclusion', 'Co-evolution of the mutualists', 'Amensalism', 'Brood parasitism'], a: 1, e: 'The evolutions of the flower and its pollinator species are tightly linked.' },
    { id: 'q141', p: ['mu3', 'mu7'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Evolution of a flower and its pollinator are tightly linked.', 'This is called co-evolution.'], a: 0, e: 'Both correct.' },
    { id: 'q142', p: ['mu4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Fig trees have a tight one-to-one relationship with their pollinator species of:', o: ['Bee', 'Wasp', 'Moth', 'Bat'], a: 1, e: 'Pollinator species of wasp (Fig 11.4).' },
    { id: 'q143', p: ['mu4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In the fig–wasp mutualism, the female wasp uses the fig fruit:', o: ['Only as food', 'As an oviposition site and its developing seeds for nourishing larvae', 'As a shelter from rain', 'For mating only'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q144', p: ['mu4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A given fig species can be pollinated only by its partner wasp species.', R: 'In return for pollination, the fig offers the wasp some developing seeds as food for its larvae.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true, but R (the reward) does not explain the one-to-one specificity, which results from tight co-evolution.' },
    { id: 'q145', p: ['mu5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Orchids show a bewildering diversity of floral patterns to:', o: ['Repel insects', 'Attract the right pollinator insect and ensure guaranteed pollination', 'Store water', 'Avoid herbivores'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT statement.' },
    { id: 'q146', p: ['mu5', 'mu6'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['All orchids offer rewards to their pollinators.', 'Ophrys employs sexual deceit to get pollinated.'], a: 3, e: 'Statement I false — not all orchids offer rewards. II true.' },
    { id: 'q147', p: ['mu6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The Mediterranean orchid that employs \'sexual deceit\' is:', o: ['Vanda', 'Ophrys', 'Dendrobium', 'Cymbidium'], a: 1, e: 'The Mediterranean orchid Ophrys.' },
    { id: 'q148', p: ['mu6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In Ophrys, one petal resembles:', o: ['The male bee', 'The female bee in size, colour and markings', 'A wasp', 'A fig'], a: 1, e: 'One petal bears an uncanny resemblance to the female of the bee.' },
    { id: 'q149', p: ['mu6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The male bee \'pseudocopulates\' with the Ophrys flower and:', o: ['Gets nectar', 'Gets dusted with pollen and transfers it to another flower', 'Lays eggs', 'Eats the petal'], a: 1, e: 'It is dusted with pollen and pollinates another flower.' },
    { id: 'q150', p: ['mu7'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The Ophrys flower co-evolves with the female bee.', R: 'If female bee\'s colour patterns change, pollination success falls unless the petal resemblance is maintained.' }, a: 0, e: 'Example of co-evolution.' }
  ]
});

addQs('bot-12-11', [
  { id: 'q151', p: ['pa2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which of the following is NOT an example of a population?', o: ['All cormorants in a wetland', 'Teakwood trees in a forest tract', 'Lotus plants in a pond', 'All the plants and animals of a pond together'], a: 3, e: 'A population is a group of individuals of the same species in an area. All plants and animals of a pond together form a community, not a population.' },
  { id: 'q152', p: ['pi2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Interspecific interactions arise between:', o: ['Individuals of the same population', 'Populations of two different species', 'Organisms and abiotic factors only', 'Male and female of the same species'], a: 1, e: 'Interspecific interactions arise from interaction of populations of two different species; they could be beneficial (+), detrimental (−) or neutral (0).' },
  { id: 'q153', p: ['pi5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The common characteristic shared by predation, parasitism and commensalism is that:', o: ['Both species are harmed', 'Both species benefit', 'The interacting species live closely together', 'One species is always unaffected'], a: 2, e: 'Predation, parasitism and commensalism share a common characteristic – the interacting species live closely together.' },
  { id: 'q154', p: ['pr4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Biological control methods adopted in agricultural pest control are based on:', o: ['Use of chemical pesticides', 'The ability of the predator to regulate prey population', 'Competitive release of the pest', 'Introduction of exotic pests'], a: 1, e: 'Biological control methods in agricultural pest control are based on the ability of the predator to regulate prey population.' },
  { id: 'q155', p: ['pt2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which of the following is NOT an adaptation of parasites?', o: ['Loss of unnecessary sense organs', 'Presence of adhesive organs or suckers', 'Loss of digestive system', 'Low reproductive capacity'], a: 3, e: 'Parasites have high reproductive capacity, along with loss of unnecessary sense organs, adhesive organs/suckers and loss of digestive system.' }
]);
